TEXT 0 -320 Left 2 ;Problem 3.3 (slides: 2.3) - tubes in a 23 L box.  Acoustic IMPEDANCE analogy, U = 1 m^3/s, so V(p_in..) = Z_A [Pa s/m^3]
TEXT 0 -272 Left 2 !.param rho=1.18 c=344 l=0.12 r1=0.05 r2=0.005 V=0.023
TEXT 0 -240 Left 2 !.param Ma1={rho*l/(pi*r1**2)} Ma2={rho*l/(pi*r2**2)} Cav={V/(rho*c**2)}
TEXT 0 -112 Left 2 ;f) wide tube + box only: plot V(p_in_f), minimum at f_A = 92.4 Hz
TEXT 0 336 Left 2 ;c-e) narrow tube added, driven from the WIDE tube: V(p_box_de) peaks at f_B = 9.2 Hz; V(p_in_de) peak 9.2 Hz, dip 92.8 Hz
TEXT 0 784 Left 2 ;g) driven from the NARROW tube: V(p_in_g) peak (Ma1 || Cav) at 92.4 Hz right next to the dip at 92.8 Hz
TEXT 0 1088 Left 2 !.ac dec 5000 1 1000
TEXT 0 1136 Left 2 ;the box compliance always goes to ground (it is the pressure inside the box, measured against the outside);
TEXT 0 1168 Left 2 ;a tube that opens to the outside also ends on ground (p_outside ~ 0, radiation impedance neglected)
